(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-11"] = {"11.1":{"s":"A freely hung bar magnet points north-south. This chapter asks why some materials are magnetic and others are not. It shows how materials respond to a magnetic field. It also covers permanent magnets, electromagnets and magnetic shields."},"11.2":{"s":"Put a magnetic dipole of moment m in a uniform field B. It feels a torque τ = mB sin θ that tries to line it up with the field. Its potential energy is U = −mB cos θ, lowest when aligned. If disturbed slightly, a freely pivoted magnet oscillates about the field direction.","f":[{"x":"tau = m*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a magnet of moment m in a uniform field B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.251"},{"x":"U_m = -m*B*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy: least (-mB) when aligned with B, most (+mB) when opposite.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.252"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(I/(m*B))","n":"Period of small angular oscillations; I = moment of inertia of the magnet.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.2, p.253"}],"w":["A stronger magnet or a stronger field gives FASTER oscillations (shorter period)."]},"11.3":{"s":"Magnetism in materials comes from electrons. An electron orbiting the nucleus is a tiny current loop with a magnetic moment, and the electron's spin adds another. In atoms where all electrons are paired, these moments cancel. Atoms with unpaired electrons have a net magnetic moment.","f":[{"x":"m_orb = (e/(2*m_e))*L","n":"Orbital magnetic moment from the orbital angular momentum L; e/(2 m_e) = gyromagnetic ratio.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.3, p.254"},{"x":"mu_B = e*h/(4*pi*m_e)","n":"Bohr magneton = 9.274 × 10^-24 A m^2, the moment of the first Bohr orbit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.3, p.254"}],"w":["The electron is negative, so its orbital magnetic moment points opposite to its angular momentum."]},"11.4":{"s":"Magnetization (M) is the net magnetic moment per unit volume of a material. Magnetic intensity (H) describes the applied field. They are linked by B = μ₀(H + M). Magnetic susceptibility χ = M/H tells how easily a material is magnetized, and the relative permeability is 1 + χ.","f":[{"x":"M = m_net/V","n":"Magnetization: net magnetic moment per unit volume (A/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.255"},{"x":"B = mu_0*(H + M)","n":"H = magnetic intensity (A/m), set by the free current, e.g. H = n I in a solenoid.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"},{"x":"M = chi*H","n":"chi = magnetic susceptibility (no unit).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"},{"x":"mu_r = 1 + chi","n":"Relative permeability; mu = mu_0 mu_r and B = mu H.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.4, p.256"}],"w":["H and M have the same unit (A/m); B is in tesla."]},"11.5":{"s":"Diamagnetic materials (copper, bismuth, water) are weakly repelled; χ is small and negative. Paramagnetic materials (aluminium, oxygen) are weakly attracted; χ is small and positive and falls as temperature rises (Curie's law). Ferromagnetic materials (iron, nickel, cobalt) are strongly attracted because of domains. Above the Curie temperature they become paramagnetic.","f":[{"x":"chi = C*mu_0/T","n":"Curie's law for a paramagnet: susceptibility falls as temperature rises.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.5, p.259"},{"x":"chi = C/(T - T_C)","n":"Ferromagnet above its Curie temperature T_C, where it behaves as a paramagnet.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 11.5, p.261"}],"w":["Diamagnetic χ is small and negative; paramagnetic small and positive; ferromagnetic very large and positive.","A diamagnetic rod sets at right angles to the field; a paramagnetic rod along it."]},"11.6":{"s":"When a ferromagnet is magnetized and demagnetized, B lags behind H. The B–H loop is called hysteresis. Retentivity is the magnetism left when H is zero. Coercivity is the reverse H needed to remove it. The area of the loop is the energy lost as heat per cycle.","w":["Retentivity is the B left when H = 0; coercivity is the reverse H needed to bring B to 0."]},"11.7":{"s":"Soft iron has high permeability and low coercivity, so it magnetizes and demagnetizes easily: good for electromagnets and transformer cores. Hard materials such as steel and alnico keep their magnetism (high retentivity and coercivity): good for permanent magnets.","w":["Electromagnet core: soft iron (thin loop, low loss). Permanent magnet: hard material (wide loop)."]},"11.8":{"s":"Put a closed shell of soft iron in a magnetic field. The field lines crowd into its walls, so very little field reaches the space inside. This magnetic shielding protects sensitive instruments, such as watches and meters, from outside fields."}};
